WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:03.568
A taxonomy sorts content into terms.

00:00:03.568 --> 00:00:07.419
A term page gives a term an address of its own, and lists

00:00:07.419 --> 00:00:09.253
the content that has it.

00:00:09.253 --> 00:00:13.960
In this video, we browse term pages and see what gives a term its page.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:22.916
Each term, such as a category of a blog, can have a page of its own.

00:00:22.916 --> 00:00:27.390
Visitors use these pages to browse your content by category or by tag.

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:32.567
The address of a term page is the permalink of the taxonomy, followed

00:00:32.567 --> 00:00:34.632
by the permalink of the term.

00:00:34.632 --> 00:00:39.590
For the term Travel in the taxonomy Categories, it is categories, then travel.

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:45.149
The page lists the content items that have the term selected in a taxonomy field.

00:00:45.149 --> 00:00:48.797
An item that has only a child term selected is not listed on

00:00:48.797 --> 00:00:50.400
the page of the parent.

00:00:54.611 --> 00:00:56.806
Here is the home page of a blog.

00:00:56.806 --> 00:01:01.197
Under each post, its category and its tags appear as badges, and every

00:01:01.197 --> 00:01:03.681
badge links to the page of that term.

00:01:04.143 --> 00:01:06.353
Let's select the Travel badge.

00:01:06.353 --> 00:01:12.063
The page that opens is the term page of Travel, at the address categories, then travel.

00:01:12.526 --> 00:01:14.798
The heading is the name of the term.

00:01:14.798 --> 00:01:19.179
Below it, the posts that have Travel selected appear newest first, each one

00:01:19.179 --> 00:01:22.316
as its summary, the way it appears on the home page.

00:01:22.767 --> 00:01:24.316
Tags work the same way.

00:01:24.316 --> 00:01:28.001
Selecting the Space badge of a post opens the term page of that

00:01:28.001 --> 00:01:30.137
tag, with the posts that carry it.

00:01:30.603 --> 00:01:34.082
A taxonomy has a page too, at its own permalink.

00:01:34.082 --> 00:01:39.301
For the Tags taxonomy, it lists the terms, and each term links to its page.

00:01:39.301 --> 00:01:40.783
Let's open Earth.

00:01:41.237 --> 00:01:44.541
The Earth page lists the three posts that have this tag.

00:01:44.541 --> 00:01:48.271
A post with several tags, such as the oldest one, appears on the

00:01:48.271 --> 00:01:49.497
page of each tag.

00:01:53.723 --> 00:01:58.041
A term has a page only when its taxonomy is set up to route it.

00:01:58.041 --> 00:02:02.483
In Content, Content Items, search for Categories and select Edit.

00:02:02.943 --> 00:02:05.970
The Permalink of the taxonomy is categories.

00:02:05.970 --> 00:02:09.663
It is the first part of the address of every term page.

00:02:10.117 --> 00:02:12.451
Route contained items is checked.

00:02:12.451 --> 00:02:16.223
It gives each term of the taxonomy an address of its own.

00:02:16.223 --> 00:02:18.737
Without it, the terms have no pages.

00:02:19.191 --> 00:02:22.265
Scrolling down, each term has an Edit button.

00:02:22.265 --> 00:02:23.712
Let's open Travel.

00:02:23.712 --> 00:02:28.171
Its Permalink is travel, the second part of the address of its page.

00:02:32.402 --> 00:02:33.998
Term pages are paged.

00:02:33.998 --> 00:02:37.839
The number of items on a page is the Page size in Settings,

00:02:37.839 --> 00:02:40.202
General, which is set to ten here.

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:43.967
Let's change the Page size to three, and select Save.

00:02:43.967 --> 00:02:47.330
A message confirms that the site settings are updated.

00:02:47.809 --> 00:02:52.249
Back on the Travel term page, the list holds only the three newest

00:02:52.249 --> 00:02:54.839
posts, and a pager appears under it.

00:02:55.307 --> 00:02:59.055
Selecting page two shows the three older posts.

00:02:59.055 --> 00:03:02.167
Every term page is paged the same way.

00:03:06.367 --> 00:03:11.448
First, the Autoroute feature is enabled, and the taxonomy has Route contained items

00:03:11.448 --> 00:03:16.757
checked, so that each of its terms gets an address under the permalink of the taxonomy.

00:03:17.507 --> 00:03:19.987
Second, the taxonomy is published.

00:03:19.987 --> 00:03:24.452
A term has a page only once the published taxonomy contains it, so

00:03:24.452 --> 00:03:27.367
publish the taxonomy after you add terms.

00:03:28.117 --> 00:03:33.039
Third, the content type of the terms has Manage contained item routes checked,

00:03:33.039 --> 00:03:35.499
so each term uses its own Permalink.

00:03:35.499 --> 00:03:40.187
Without it, the address ends with the identifier of the term and its name.

00:03:40.937 --> 00:03:45.345
Finally, content items need a taxonomy field with the term selected.

00:03:45.345 --> 00:03:50.587
The term page lists those items, and a separate video covers how to add the field.

00:03:52.587 --> 00:03:56.171
A term page gives each term an address and a paged list of

00:03:56.171 --> 00:03:57.851
the content that has it.

00:03:57.851 --> 00:03:59.307
Thanks for watching.

